      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): prevent double dispatch after disable/enable cycle during in-flight run",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nDisabling and re-enabling a cron job while a run is still in flight cleared the persisted `runningAtMs` reservation. The next scheduler tick then saw a past-due `nextRunAtMs` with no concurrency guard and dispatched a second overlapping run.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThree cooperating owners keep the in-flight guard intact:\n\n1. `finalizeUpdatedJob` — on disable, keep `runningAtMs` **only while** `isCronJobActive(job.id)` so queued force reservations can still clear and be replaced (same-millisecond admission).\n2. `normalizeJobTickState` — for disabled jobs, clear `runningAtMs` only when stuck **and** not a queued force reservation (`isQueuedForceCronRun`).\n3. `isRunnableJob` — also reject when `isCronJobActive(job.id)`.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators who disable/re-enable a job during an active run no longer get a second concurrent execution of that job. Force re-run after disable (queued admission) keeps working.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| Layer | Proof |\n| --- | --- |\n| Head | `29f692e794f6110ea6d982a02ffe7fb310a6e945` (on `main` tip rebuild + CI fix) |\n| Scope | 4 files, +83 / −7 |\n| L1 | `ops.regression.test.ts` `#102238` — mid-run disable→enable→`onTimer` stays single dispatch |\n| L1 sibling | `ops.run-admission` same-ms replacement after disable must keep working (CI regression that failed when disable always preserved `runningAtMs`) |\n| CI fix | Preserve `runningAtMs` on disable only if `isCronJobActive`; oxfmt on touched files |\n\nFixes #102238",
        "branch": "fix/cron-disable-enable-double-dispatch",
